# Freeze Boundary Switchover Handoff Notes

When this page was first written, the path looked straightforward: count the consumers, pick one to try the candidate path, then move the remaining entry points after it succeeds. The first review kept that sequence, but found that "counting them all" was not the same as copying names into a list. Each known consumer must be traceable back to the old entry point it actually depends on, with the revision used for this review and a minimal reproduction recorded.

## Materials Before Review

Before starting on the candidate path, freeze the state of the old entry point. The record must include more than the entry point's name; it must also identify the first visible divergence. That divergence may be in path selection, or at the point where the caller cannot continue; do not fill in unknowns with "looks compatible." Alternative evidence without a corresponding caller, revision, and reproduction cannot be used as evidence that the migration is complete.

Handoff cards now record the following, in order:

- Consumer: the known entity; leave as unknown if it cannot be found;
- Old entry point: the entry point this consumer actually depends on now;
- revision: the version of the object pinned for this review;
- Minimal reproduction: the minimum material needed to reproduce the first divergence;
- First divergence: the point where the old entry point and the candidate path first differ;
- Next-step entry point: an entry point the person taking over can use to continue execution;
- Rollback method: the action this consumer can take to restore the old entry point on its own.

"Consumer" is not shorthand for a project name. If the caller population has not been confirmed, leave this line as unknown; do not fill it in with material from neighboring projects. Only one consumer that can roll back on its own gets to try the candidate path; all other known consumers continue using the old entry point. If it fails, the impact can therefore still be contained within a single local boundary.

## Post-Review Handling

First check the revision and minimal reproduction, then examine the first divergence. Proceed to the next batch only if the divergence can be explained and the rollback action is still available. A successful local trial only shows that the participant in that trial produced a result; it does not automatically prove that other participants can switch. If the caller population has not been recorded, keep the page marked as migration in progress.

Rollback does not mean deleting the candidate path. The consumer running the trial restores its original entry point, while recording the first divergence seen before the failure and the revision used. After restoration, check the minimal reproduction and rollback result as well; if either is missing, the status must not be marked as switched over.

Keep records separate across surfaces: Board records only the status and next step, without copying the full reproduction; Docs maintains the long-term version of the guidance on this page; Chat records observations made at the time, confirmations still pending, and issues that need referral. All three link back only to the same event. DOC-01 remain local material related to the old entry point and cannot replace this handoff card.
